Returns true only when the current HTTP request method is POST.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->posted()) {
    echo 'Form submitted';
}
```

### Notes

- This is a convenience check for POST submissions.

Returns true when the current HTTP request method is GET.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->is_get()) {
    echo 'Page loaded normally';
}
```

### Notes

- Useful for separating normal page loads from submitted forms.

Detects AJAX requests by inspecting the HTTP_X_REQUESTED_WITH header.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->is_ajax()) {
    echo 'AJAX request detected';
}
```

### Notes

- Detection depends on the `HTTP_X_REQUESTED_WITH` header.

Reads one POST value with a fallback, or returns the full POST array when no key is supplied.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$name = $req->post('name');
$status = $req->post('status', 1);
$all_posted = $req->post();
```

### Notes

- Calling without a key returns the complete POST array.
- Key-existence checks preserve valid values such as `0` and `'0'` instead of treating them as missing.

Convenience alias for reading posted input with a default fallback.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$email = $req->input('email');
$role = $req->input('role', 'user');
```

### Notes

- This is an alias for posted input rather than a combined GET/POST lookup.
- It preserves valid zero values.

Reads one query-string value with a fallback, or returns the full GET array when no key is supplied.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$page = (int) $req->get('page', 1);
$sort = $req->get('sort', 'latest');
$keyword = trim($req->get('q', ''));
```

### Notes

- Calling without a key returns the complete GET array.
- Common uses include pagination, filters, search terms, and sorting.
- Valid zero values are preserved.

Reads one value or the complete data set from REQUEST.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$data = $req->all();
$tab = $req->all('tab', 'general');
```

### Notes

- Reads from `$_REQUEST`, which may combine GET, POST, and, depending on PHP configuration, cookies.
- Prefer `post()` or `get()` when the expected source is known so controller behavior remains explicit.

Checks whether a POST key exists without treating valid zero values as missing.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->has_post('email')) {
    echo 'Email field was submitted';
}
```

### Notes

- This checks whether the key exists, not whether its value is non-empty.

Checks whether a GET key exists without treating valid zero values as missing.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->has_get('page')) {
    echo 'Page parameter exists';
}
```

### Notes

- This checks whether the key exists, not whether its value is non-empty.

Checks whether a single or array-style file input contains at least one uploaded file.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

if ($req->has_file('photos')) {
    echo 'At least one photo exists';
}
```

### Notes

- A FILES entry does not always mean the user selected a file; this helper checks the upload content.
- It also supports inputs such as `photos[]`.

Returns raw uploaded-file data from FILES for one field or all fields.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$avatar = $req->files('avatar');
$all_files = $req->files();
```

### Notes

- This exposes PHP file-array data before upload processing.
- Single and multiple file inputs have different raw shapes; the upload processor normalizes them internally.

Registers a callback for custom upload filename generation.

### Example

```php
$req->set_filename_callback(function (
    $original_name,
    $safe_name,
    $extension,
    $field_name,
    $file_info
) {
    $id = rand(1000, 9999);
    return $field_name . '_' . $id . '.' . $extension;
});
```

### Notes

- The callback receives original name, safe base name, extension, field name, and file information.
- Return a safe and unique filename; the built-in randomized naming is the safer default for most cases.

Returns true when validation, folder, or move errors were collected during upload processing.

### Example

```php
$req->upload_files('avatar');

if ($req->has_upload_errors()) {
    print_r($req->get_upload_errors());
}
```

### Notes

- Errors include validation failures, move failures, and system-level folder problems.

Reads one SERVER value with a fallback, or returns all server data when no key is supplied.

### Example

```php
$req = new \Core\Request();

$agent = $req->server('HTTP_USER_AGENT');
$all_server_data = $req->server();
```

### Notes

- Call without a key to return all SERVER data.
- Provide a fallback for optional keys.
